  <p>
    The 1.6.0 package from release PR #314 was tested in virtual machines on 26 October 2026 on
    Ubuntu 26.04 (GNOME Wayland, <code>resolute</code> package) and Linux Mint 22 (Cinnamon X11,
    <code>noble</code> package), using Gõ Sen and Telex. For the upgrade, each machine had 1.5.8-1
    installed, with one setting and one macro saved, before running install.sh with the new package;
    fcitx5 reloaded automatically, the setting, macro and Ctrl+Space were kept, and Cứu chữ was enabled.
    Then, in a GTK3 text field on both machines: the sentence
    <code>dduwowngf tieengs Vieetj Nam cos dduwowjc khoong Haf Nooij</code> produced the correct text
    with tone marks; typing <code>tieengs vieetj</code> in English and switching Vietnamese on let
    Cứu chữ convert it to <code>tiếng việt</code>, and Esc restored the original; typing
    <code>tieengs Nam</code> and switching Vietnamese on only showed a suggestion, and Tab kept the
    original text. On Ubuntu, those four cases also passed in Chrome, and the sentence typed correctly
    in GNOME Console; on Mint, the sentence typed correctly in Chrome. A fresh install (with the build
    immediately before upgrade fix #315, which did not touch the fresh-install path) opened in English,
    Ctrl+Shift switched to Vietnamese, and Cứu chữ was enabled by default. Fedora, Arch, Debian,
